# HTTP caching (Cloudflare Pages).
#
# Rules ACCUMULATE — they do not override. Per Cloudflare's docs: "An incoming
# request which matches multiple rules' URL patterns will inherit all rules'
# headers", and "if a header is applied twice in the _headers file, the values
# are joined with a comma separator".
#
# So an asset rule cannot simply restate Cache-Control: /* still contributes
# its own value and the two get joined into one nonsense header
# ("max-age=0, must-revalidate, public, max-age=3600"). Each asset block first
# detaches the inherited value with "! Cache-Control".
#
# For the same reason X-Content-Type-Options is declared once, on /*, and
# inherited everywhere else. Repeating it would produce "nosniff, nosniff".

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# CSS/JS share a contract with the HTML (class names, data-tags), and the HTML
# revalidates on every visit — so keep these short enough that the two cannot
# drift far apart. Filenames are not fingerprinted, so this is the only brake.
# Re-checking costs one round trip and returns a bodyless 304 when unchanged.
#
# Matching on the extension rather than the folder also covers the exhibition
# mini-sites, which keep their stylesheet inside their own folder — Cloudflare's
# * spans path separators, so /*.css reaches
# /exhibitions/origin-seoul-2026/origin-seoul-2026.css too. Their HTML keeps
# revalidating like every other page.
/*.css
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/*.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Safe to cache forever ONLY because these bytes never change under a fixed
# name — see the warning in scripts/build-images.sh before replacing a photo.
/img/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The favicons are tiny and near-static; without a rule they fall through to
# /* and revalidate on every navigation, costing a round trip per page view.
# A month, not a year, and not immutable: unlike an artwork photo these are not
# renamed when they change, so staleness has to stay bounded.
/*.svg
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000
